---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { url } from '../../../utils/url';
---
<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Understanding LIVEX"
  description="LIVEX in plain terms: what a multi-agent simulation ecosystem is for, how it works, and which limitations it accepts."
>
  <PageHeader
    lang="en"
    index="01"
    eyebrow="Understand"
    title="A living archive of a simulated world."
    lead="LIVEX starts from a simple question: can a collective behaviour that no one programmed by hand be made observable? What the project produces is not a story, but a record."
    crumbs={[{ label: 'Understand' }]}
  />

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>1.1</b> The problem</div>
      <h2>Understand without scripting.</h2>
      <p>
        Many simulations settle for playing out a planned sequence. You see in them exactly what was put in. LIVEX aims
        for the opposite: place agents, rules and an environment, then <strong>let it run</strong> and record what
        happens — including the unexpected, including what does not work.
      </p>
      <p>
        This imposes two requirements. First, <strong>determinism</strong>: from the same seed, a run must replay
        identically, tick after tick. Then, <strong>separation</strong>: what happens (the engine) and what is said
        about it (the observation) must never be conflated, on pain of narrating what one hoped to see.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>1.2</b> The method</div>
      <h2>Three steps, three components.</h2>
      <div class="g3 br">
        <div>
          <div class="n">01</div>
          <h3>Simulate</h3>
          <p><strong>SYNE</strong> runs the world: agents, resources, rules. Every run is replayable identically and
            timestamped by a seed.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="n">02</div>
          <h3>Observe</h3>
          <p><strong>ECHOS</strong> ingests the traces and derives measurements from them: viability, patterns, loops.
            It observes without acting on the world.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="n">03</div>
          <h3>Make visible</h3>
          <p><strong>PRISM</strong> makes the world and its dynamics perceptible, through rendering and interaction.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="w">
      <div class="sh"><b>1.3</b> What LIVEX is not</div>
      <h2>Limitations we accept.</h2>
      <div class="cc">
        <p>
          LIVEX's agents are neither conscious, nor people, nor models of real societies. They are
          <strong>simulated</strong> entities, whose “emotions” or “intentions” are variables, not mental states. LIVEX
          claims neither to predict nor to reproduce reality.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The project is in an exploration phase (V0.1). No public version is distributed to date. The component states
        describe the development repository.
      </p>
      <div class="toc" style="margin-top:22px">
        <a href={url('comprendre/limites/', 'en')}>Limitations and scope →</a>
        <a href={url('comprendre/emergence/', 'en')}>Emergence explained →</a>
        <a href={url('comprendre/glossaire/', 'en')}>Glossary →</a>
        <a href={url('technique/', 'en')}>Architecture →</a>
        <a href={url('faq/', 'en')}>FAQ →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
